WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.120
The love between Lois Lane and Superman has&nbsp;
spanned across 80 years of DC comics — so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.840
naturally, things have gotten a&nbsp;
little weird every now and again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.320
From messed-up marriages to role-play gone wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.080
here are some of the strangest things about&nbsp;
Lois Lane and Superman's relationship.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.640
Smallville takes the unconventional route to&nbsp;
romance when Lois Lane appears in the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.320
fourth season. Let's just say that meeting&nbsp;
your amnesiac future lover while they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.200
standing naked in a burning field isn't exactly&nbsp;
the meet-cute dreams are made of. Unless you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.160
into that kind of thing, which, as it turns&nbsp;
out, Clark Kent and Lois Lane definitely are.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.600
It all goes down in the first episode&nbsp;
of season four, when Lois shows up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.040
investigate the death of her cousin.&nbsp;
During her road trip, she winds up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.200
a cornfield — where she discovers Clark in&nbsp;
the nude, lying in a burned patch of corn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.240
When Supes gets up on his feet, he insists that&nbsp;
he's fine — to which Lois quite rightly answers:

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.840
"You've just been hit by&nbsp;
lightning, you're stark naked, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.080
you don't even remember your own name. You&nbsp;
have a fairly loose definition of 'fine.'"

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.840
Lois and Clark's will they/won't&nbsp;
they flirtations last for five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.480
whole seasons on the show, and&nbsp;
even after they start dating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.640
there's something awkward about all their&nbsp;
banter and palpable sexual tension. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.880
never gets quite as weird as that day they ran&nbsp;
into each other in that half-scorched cornfield.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.960
Having a kid and saving the world&nbsp;
at the same time is no easy feat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.560
Finding out that your children multiplied&nbsp;
while you were out saving the world is even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.560
harder — but that's exactly what went down&nbsp;
with the Arrowverse version of Superman.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.080
This version of Lois Lane makes her first&nbsp;
appearance in the Arrowverse crossover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.400
episode "Elseworlds," in which Lois and Clark&nbsp;
reveal that they're expecting a baby. Cut to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.160
"Crisis on Infinite Earths" a year later,&nbsp;
where the couple have one child together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.000
called Jonathan. Unfortunately, the&nbsp;
notorious villain known as the Anti-Monitor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.000
is busy destroying multiple Earths in the&nbsp;
multiverse. When everything's said and done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.320
the gang can only save one Earth — and&nbsp;
reality as they know it is altered forever.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.560
At the end of the story, the superhero J'onn&nbsp;
J'onzz restores the pre-Crisis memories of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.600
allies — but things are kinda different in this&nbsp;
strange new version of Earth, especially for those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.400
who remember how things used to be. Take Superman,&nbsp;
for example. Clark, who began the day with one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:05.520
child, is kinda surprised during a phone call&nbsp;
with Lois when she mentions their "sons," plural.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.000
"Clark, I need you to get to&nbsp;
Metropolis now. It's the boys."

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:10.320
"The boys?"

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.480
"Yeah, honey, your sons."

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.160
Congrats, Clark — again.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.800
Everyone always looks forward to the&nbsp;
supercharged romantic climax of a love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.800
story, when the couple finally kisses. At&nbsp;
first glance, it seems that Superman II&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.120
provides that moment with gusto… until you&nbsp;
realize what's actually going on, that is.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.320
First introduced in Action Comics #306, Superman&nbsp;
has occasionally demonstrated his ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.800
mind-wipe people with a kiss. The concept&nbsp;
of messing with someone's memory is shady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.680
even with random bystanders, but doing it to&nbsp;
someone you supposedly love and trust is much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.560
worse. Instead of kissing Lois for the right&nbsp;
reasons in the 1980 film, Superman uses his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.120
amnesia trick as a power move to alter Lois'&nbsp;
memory when she figures out his secret identity.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.960
Given that knowing Superman's secret doesn't&nbsp;
seem to change how Lois feels about him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.440
his actions come off that much crueler. Even&nbsp;
worse, if Superman intends to remain close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.000
with Lois in both his forms, she'll likely just&nbsp;
discover the truth all over again; after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.320
this is super sleuth Lois Lane we're talking&nbsp;
about here. And if she does figure it out again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.480
chances are she'll put together the fact that&nbsp;
Superman messed with her mind — something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:17.840
won't exactly go over well. Superman's gonna have&nbsp;
a lot of explaining to do after that little stunt.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.400
When you're as coveted a hero as Superman,&nbsp;
sometimes one of you just isn't enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.600
go around. Luckily, within the&nbsp;
panels of the comic book world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:31.120
anything is possible — even splitting&nbsp;
yourself in two. And in Superman #162,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.440
Supes does the unthinkable. He separates&nbsp;
into a red and blue version of himself.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.920
Naturally, this has consequences when it comes&nbsp;
to Clark's love life, with both Lois and Lana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:44.160
another of Superman's major love interests,&nbsp;
marrying their own version of the Man of Steel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.200
Lois settles down with the red version of Superman&nbsp;
on a newly restored Krypton, while Lana weds blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.520
Supes, ultimately chilling out on Earth. It's&nbsp;
not exactly the most conventional arrangement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.080
but since when has Superman settled for anything&nbsp;
less than five shades past extra? And sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.000
polygamy may not be everyone's cup of&nbsp;
tea, but hey — if it works, it works.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.680
Any fan of Smallville will have&nbsp;
felt the pain of watching Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.520
Rosenbaum's Lex Luthor try and fail&nbsp;
to be the best version of himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:13.440
But what if Lex really could really turn&nbsp;
good? That's gotta be impossible, right?

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:13.940
"Wrong!"

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.640
In Superman's Girl Friend Lois Lane #34,&nbsp;
Lex kidnaps Lois — as most supervillains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.880
tend to do on any given weekday. However,&nbsp;
as Lex makes his usual threats against her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:28.400
the aliens on the planet Omark set the&nbsp;
supervillain on the straight and narrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.720
curing him of all evildoing. He and Lois&nbsp;
then strike up an unlikely friendship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.120
and the reformed villain even asks her to&nbsp;
marry him. Lois initially declines, but when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.360
Lex turns up to save her from trouble instead&nbsp;
of Superman, she begins to rethink her decision.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.440
Eventually, Lois falls for her old captor,&nbsp;
and she finally accepts his proposal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.680
Of course, Superman, being the charmer that he is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:51.040
is more than happy to move onto his&nbsp;
second choice: poor old Lana Lang.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.000
Everyone knows there's nothing like a good&nbsp;
fairy tale — but you can always trust a comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.400
book to make things weird. Take&nbsp;
Action Comics #243, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.160
in which Superman and Lois act out their&nbsp;
own version of Beauty and the Beast.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:08.800
Superman gets cursed by a sorceress named Circe,&nbsp;
who slips a potion into his drink in an attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.040
to extort him into marrying her. He refuses&nbsp;
her, of course, convinced that his Kryptonian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.760
makeup is impervious to her sorcery, only to&nbsp;
find himself swiftly turned into a super lion.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.720
Lois then proves that her love&nbsp;
for Superman is thicker than fur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.720
kissing him during a fairly awkward date&nbsp;
night. And, because subtlety isn't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.280
a thing in comic books, the actual Beauty and&nbsp;
the Beast plays out on-stage at the same time.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.040
Sadly, unlike the fairy tale couple, this kiss&nbsp;
doesn't magically solve Superman's bestial woes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.920
He forlornly accepts his fate and resolves himself&nbsp;
to hanging out at the zoo — until he realizes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:44.800
Circe laced her elixir with kryptonite. As a&nbsp;
result, he's able to figure out the cure… but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.280
still lets Lois have her moment by thinking it&nbsp;
was her kiss that did the trick. What a cutie.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:53.760
When you're doing the ol' will they/won't&nbsp;
they dance with an award-winning journalist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.840
chances are good they're going to ask questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.720
no matter how well you hide&nbsp;
yourself behind a pair of glasses.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:02.480
However, despite both Clark and Superman&nbsp;
spending plenty of time with Lois,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.760
he still tends to punish her whenever&nbsp;
she gets close to uncovering his secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.800
identity — and sometimes things&nbsp;
can get a little out of hand.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.600
The debut issue of Superman's Girl Friend, Lois&nbsp;
Lane starts off on a fairly cringey note that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.160
sets the tone for the whole series. This issue&nbsp;
follows Lois as she dons her own disguise as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:23.360
successful actress to dupe Supes into agreeing&nbsp;
to an interview. Superman, the king of disguise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.760
is somehow offended by Lois' own use of&nbsp;
deception. So, to teach her a lesson, the caped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:32.000
hero dons his Clark Kent disguise and pretends&nbsp;
to jump off of a building right in front of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.600
terrified reporter. Of course, Superman assures a&nbsp;
traumatized Lois that he saved Clark just in time.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:42.000
Obviously, Supes could only have been crueller&nbsp;
to Lois here if he had pushed her off that ledge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.200
And things don't get much better from&nbsp;
there, either — throughout the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:49.760
Superman strives to find new and twisted ways to&nbsp;
torture Lois for getting too close to the truth.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:56.080
In Superman's Girl Friend, Lois Lane #48, Lois has&nbsp;
the opportunity to uncover Superman's identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.240
as the Man of Steel recorded his secret on&nbsp;
a tape he gave Perry White — which is only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.880
to be played in the event of his death. Tempted&nbsp;
to listen prematurely, Lois eventually decides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:09.680
to do the right thing, and Superman overhears.&nbsp;
So he "rewards" Lois by offering her a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.760
to guess his fabricated secret identity&nbsp;
in three time periods of his choosing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:17.920
As a result, the Kryptonian transforms her&nbsp;
into Helen of Troy, Florence Nightingale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:22.560
and Cinderella — and it's with that last&nbsp;
one that Superman really crosses the line.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.400
What seems like an amusing game turns&nbsp;
sour when Superman disguises himself as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.920
Cinderella's evil stepmother. He&nbsp;
subsequently begins abusing her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:33.920
forcing her to clean, mocking her appearance,&nbsp;
and generally treating her like a slave.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.080
Think about it: Superman had countless potential&nbsp;
characters to role-play, but he chooses the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.880
abusive option just to throw Lois off the scent.&nbsp;
Given that the game was his idea to begin with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:47.120
it's fair to say that no one forced him to go to&nbsp;
such extremes to hide his identity. He could've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:51.440
picked any time, place, and character to emulate,&nbsp;
but he went with the option to belittle and harm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:56.654
Lois. Role-play is all well and good if everyone's&nbsp;
having fun, but this is practically unhinged.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.040
Superman takes the whole "'til&nbsp;
death do us part" vow a little too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:06.000
literally when he marries Lois' dead body&nbsp;
in Superman's Girl Friend Lois Lane #103.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.960
The tale begins in the previous issue after Lois&nbsp;
meets a shifty man during an exotic animal parade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:14.720
After drinking the stranger's magical&nbsp;
elixir, Lois spouts horns, a tail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:18.960
and hooves. Needless to say, Clark Kent&nbsp;
isn't happy about this, but Lois ends up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:23.280
in the crossfire when Superman confronts the&nbsp;
mysterious figure masquerading as the devil.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:28.240
During their fight, a wayward laser blasts off&nbsp;
of Superman's chest, apparently killing Lois.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:32.640
Of course, Superman blames himself. In a fit&nbsp;
of despair, the Kryptonian finally admits his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:36.800
love for Lois and cries out wedding vows&nbsp;
over her assumedly dead body — which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.960
let's face it, probably isn't the romantic&nbsp;
wedding Lois envisioned for herself.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:45.840
Luckily, as it turns out, Lois was never dead&nbsp;
after all. The devious devil turns out to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:49.600
an alien, who takes her to his homeworld in&nbsp;
the hopes of convincing her to marry him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:53.280
She then sees the ring Superman put&nbsp;
on her finger and demands to go home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.040
Who would've thought that Clark's post-mortem&nbsp;
wedding vows would save the day after all?

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:01.680
Following Lois' ongoing trend of tricking&nbsp;
Superman into marrying her, the pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:06.560
enter into a whole new realm of weirdness&nbsp;
during Superman's Girl Friend Lois Lane #42.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:11.120
After Lois snaps a picture of Superman&nbsp;
mid-change, a car crash alters her personality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:14.640
and as a result, she gives Superman&nbsp;
a choice — marry her the next day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.360
or she'll develop the film. Obviously, every&nbsp;
great romance starts out with a blackmail scheme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.520
and things go predictably well here, too.&nbsp;
While attempting to stall the reporter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:26.720
Superman flies her to the Fortress&nbsp;
of Solitude, but when she arrives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:31.280
she spritzs herself with a dose of "rejuvenation&nbsp;
spray" and begins to rapidly de-age.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:34.320
The age difference prevents the pair&nbsp;
from acquiring a marriage license,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:37.520
but that doesn't stop Lois. She&nbsp;
sprays Superman with the serum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:41.120
and they both continue to de-age in&nbsp;
quick bursts. Eventually, they get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.600
the go-ahead to proceed with the marriage&nbsp;
— despite basically looking like toddlers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:50.400
Then, by the time the wedding rolls around, both&nbsp;
parties are literally babies. Still, everyone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:54.880
chill with moving ahead for some reason... until&nbsp;
it turns out that Lois and Superman are unable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.880 --> 00:10:00.640
to say "I do" to each other, since they can only&nbsp;
speak in baby babble. Look, it was the '60s, okay?

